    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d473">

                <pb facs="0614" n="496" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg496"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/794</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Legation in China to
                        the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs</hi><anchor
                        n="36" xml:id="d473-f1"/></head>
                <p rendition="#flush">[No.] 00548</p>
                <p>The American Legation presents its compliments to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs
                    and has the honor to inform that Ministry of the receipt by it of letters from
                    the American Chambers of Commerce in Peking and Tientsin, in which those bodies
                    point out that the Luxury and Special Articles Tax, which it is proposed to put
                    in force in Peking, will contravene the provisions of the treaties now in force
                    and will adversely affect American trade. Accordingly, the American Legation has
                    the honor very earnestly to request that the intention to put into effect this
                    tax, which will injure American trade and violate existing treaty rights of
                    American merchants, be abandoned.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peking</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1928-01-20"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">January 20, 1928</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d473-f1" n="36" type="source" xml:id="d473-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Minister in China, without covering
                    despatch; received April 6.</p>
            </note>
